 "stop": "20 lines",
 "poem": "The Mississippi is flowing,\nAnd the west wind whistles softly\nIn the valley of Pueblo, Mexico,\nAs it goes into the wilderness.  It\nIs a solitary river,\nWhere neither man nor beast has ever\nBeen seen in its flood waters.\nThere are no forests to draw our\nBy the willow-boughs along,\nNo wildflowers by the river\nShine like those around in Ohio;  but\nFar off, there grows an ocean-woodland,  where\nHuge trees stretch their limbs upward\nInto the sky and upward,\nAnd the green branches flutter\nAnd scatter white berries down upon\nThe grasses among our\nDesert Sand-hills that lie empty and\nEmpty as if nothing ever\nWas ever taken away.",
